    "title": "Concept: Structure and Role of Red Blood Cells",
    "explanation": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">Red blood cells (RBCs), also called erythrocytes, are biconcave disc-shaped cells without a nucleus. This unique shape increases their surface area to volume ratio, aiding the transport of oxygen. They contain haemoglobin, a protein that binds oxygen in the lungs and releases it to body tissues.</p>",
